--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -23,4 +23,36 @@ resolvePointer(doc, "");                   // the whole document
 - Only own properties of objects are members, so inherited names such as `/constructor` do not resolve.
 - Invalid pointers and pointers that do not resolve both throw a `JsonPointerError`, which is exported too.
 
+## JSON Patch
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a JSON Patch ([RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902)), an array of
+operations, to a JSON document and returns the resulting document:
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+const doc = { order: { items: [{ sku: "A-1" }] } };
+applyPatch(doc, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/order/items/-", value: { sku: "B-2" } },
+  { op: "test", path: "/order/items/0/sku", value: "A-1" },
+  { op: "replace", path: "/order/items/0/sku", value: "A-9" },
+]);
+// { order: { items: [{ sku: "A-9" }, { sku: "B-2" }] } }
+```
+
+The six operations are `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`. Paths are JSON Pointers, as in
+`resolvePointer`.
+
+- The input is never mutated. The result is a new value that shares no structure with the document or the patch, so
+  the caller can change it freely.
+- The patch is checked as a whole before any operation runs. A failing operation throws, and nothing is returned, so a
+  caller never receives a half-patched document.
+- Invalid patches and failing operations throw a `JsonPatchError`, which is exported too. A pointer error inside an
+  operation is reported as a `JsonPatchError` whose `cause` is the `JsonPointerError`.
+- `add` into an object replaces an existing member; `add` into an array inserts at the index, and `-` appends.
+- `move` cannot move a value into one of its own children. `remove` cannot remove the whole document.
+- `test` compares JSON values: object members match in any order, array elements in order.
+- An operation without a `value` (or a `from`, for `move` and `copy`) is invalid. `undefined` is not a JSON value and
+  is rejected as a value.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test`.
